<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Third Indochina War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1978">1978</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> War is the name given to the conflicts that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodian-vietnamese-war">Vietnamese invasion of Cambodia</a> in December 1978. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> regime and occupied the country for a decade, fighting Khmer Rouge and other resistance groups, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> attacked northern Vietnam in 1979 and border fighting continued through the 1980s. Vietnamese troops withdrew in 1989, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-agreements">Paris Peace Agreements</a> of 1991 ended the war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/third-indochina-war.jpg" alt="Third Indochina War" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Don-kun, NordNordWest · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indochina_1979_map_de.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war grew out of the rivalry between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> in 1953, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a>'s denunciation of Stalin and his policy of peaceful coexistence with the West angered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-soviet-split">Sino-Soviet split</a> deepened as both powers competed for influence among the new states and factions of the 1960s. North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> allied with the Soviet Union, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> communists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> eventually allied with China.</p><p>When American forces withdrew and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a> fell in April 1975, followed by a communist takeover in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> five months later, the common enemy of the communist powers was gone. Armed clashes soon broke out on the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> forces advanced deep into Vietnamese territory and raided villages, killing hundreds of civilians. Relations between Vietnam and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> grew tense after Vietnam chose a pro-Soviet course on unification in 1976.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Vietnamese troops reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a> in January 1979 and the Thai border that spring, ending the Cambodian genocide and installing a government of opponents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pol-pot">Pol Pot</a>, most notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hun-sen">Hun Sen</a>, a former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> commander. The remaining Khmer Rouge settled in the Thai-Cambodian border region and received support from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>'s enemies, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> tolerated their presence. Vietnamese forces attacked their bases inside Thailand, and Thai and Vietnamese regular troops clashed several times with heavy casualties. An anti-Vietnamese coalition government was formed in 1982.</p><p>In February 1979 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> launched a punitive attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>'s northern provinces and captured several cities near the border; on 6 March 1979 it declared its mission a success and withdrew, though both sides claimed victory. Border disputes and naval clashes between the two armies continued until 1990 without lasting gains for either.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Isolated except for the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and its allies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> became bogged down in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>. By the late 1980s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-vietnam">Vietnamese Communist Party</a> had adopted its Doi Moi policy and judged that hostility with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> harmed its economic reforms, opening the way to normalisation in 1991. The 1991 Paris Peace Accords led to a transition and the restoration of a multi-party <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> in Cambodia in 1993. Related conflicts included the insurgency in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, the Thai-Laotian Border War, the Johnson South Reef Skirmish and the FULRO insurgency against Vietnam.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Third Indochina War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its occupation of Cambodia ended the Khmer Rouge regime that had killed well over a million Cambodians, but it was condemned by ASEAN, China and the West, and the resulting <b>Third Indochina War</b> kept the region divided until the Paris Peace Agreements on Cambodia of 1991.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
